What Changed in v2.0

Version 1.0 contained four deliberate pedagogical traps (cycles vs ns, memory math, cache determinism, RTOS tick budget) and several accidental faults (0x-AMBIG, undefined functions, API name errors, unit mismatches). Version 2.0 fixes all faults and labels the traps explicitly so readers know the difference between a lesson and a typo. If you want the trap version for teaching, see Appendix D.

---

1. Introduction

ST-T1024 is a deterministic AI inference architecture for embedded and edge hardware. This guide provides a practical, step-by-step path to implementing ST-T1024 on a microcontroller or microprocessor — ARM, RISC-V, or otherwise.

The core insight: ST-T1024 eliminates floating-point multipliers, stochastic sampling, and large DRAM arrays. This dramatically reduces silicon and firmware complexity, making it accessible to any competent embedded engineer.

Key architectural metrics:

Metric Value Notes
Cycle budget (WCET) 800 cycles Architectural constant
Time budget @ 1 GHz 800 ns Silicon target
Time budget @ 200 MHz 4 µs MCU prototype
Energy per query 3.7 µJ Measured on Snapdragon 8 Gen 4
Memory (tile working set) 700 B 525 B ROM + 175 B SRAM
Arithmetic Ternary {−1, 0, +1} XNOR + popcount, no multipliers
Power gating (silicon) < 5 ns wake Requires on-die switches
Power gating (MCU) ~1 µs wake Clock gating via PMIC

Note on timing. The 800 ns figure is a silicon target at 1 GHz. On an MCU at 200 MHz, the same 800-cycle budget takes 4 µs. The architecture is identical; only the clock differs. Every timing claim in this guide is stated in cycles first, and translated to nanoseconds only where the clock is known.

---

2. Architecture Overview

The six-step deterministic pipeline:

Step Component Function
1 Intent Harvesting Normalize input, compute routing hash
2 FIS Triage Fuzzy rule set — route to correct tile
3 FSM Scheduling Enforce cycle budget, manage state
4 Tile Activation Power-on only the target tile
5 Execution Ternary transformer inference
6 Power-Down Return to 0 V idle

The FSM cycle counter is hardware-enforced. If a tile exceeds its budget, execution halts and the FSM enters STATE_ERROR with ERR_WCET. This is the Assured Execution Time (AET) guarantee.

