7. Timing, Determinism, and What Breaks Them

7.1 The Cycle Budget

WCET_CYCLES = 800 is the architectural constant. Time depends on clock:

Clock WCET AET (measured 680 cycles)
1 GHz (silicon) 800 ns 680 ns
480 MHz (Cortex-M7 max) 1.67 µs 1.42 µs
200 MHz (typical MCU) 4.0 µs 3.4 µs
100 MHz (minimum) 8.0 µs 6.8 µs

The architecture is unchanged. Only the wall-clock number moves.

7.2 Why Caches Break WCET

D-cache hit/miss timing depends on access history, which depends on input, which varies. WCET analysis with caches is an open research problem. For ST-T1024, disable D-cache or use TCM exclusively. This is not optional.

7.3 Why RTOS Breaks WCET

A tick-based RTOS cannot schedule at sub-microsecond granularity. A 1 ms tick is 1,000,000 ns — 1,250× the 800 ns budget. If you must use an RTOS, ST-T1024 runs as the highest-priority non-preemptible task, and the AET guarantee is suspended while any other task runs. For certified use, bare metal only.

7.4 Why Floats Break Determinism (Eventually)

float and double arithmetic is deterministic on a given core, but the rounding behaviour differs between ARM FPU, RISC-V F/D extension, and soft-float libraries. A tile developed on one platform will not produce bit-identical results on another. ST-T1024 uses ternary integers precisely to avoid this class of cross-platform divergence.

